{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSBtoFN67AAnNdgFpmD5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've fixed three separate bugs in the Intervals.icu connection, and each one was enough on its own to block athletes. The full flow passed against a fake Intervals server on my machine. I couldn't test against the real intervals.icu, so the scope fix (cause 2) is a deduction from the logs, not something I've seen succeed.\n\n1. **The Connect button never sent the sign-in token.** The button was a plain link, and the token only goes out from the app's own requests to the server. Clicking the link didn't send it, so every athlete got `401 unauthorized`. Now the button asks the server for the Intervals sign-in link, with the token attached, and then opens it (`static/app.js`, `server.py`).\n\n2. **Intervals rejected the list of permissions we ask for.** When calendar sync shipped on 2026-10-02, the list started naming the same area twice, e.g. `CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`. Intervals expects each area once, and write access already includes read. Rivera's grant from before that change came back as just `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`, which fits. It's now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`, which gives the same access (`config.py`). That's why pasting the link by hand started failing too.\n\n3. **Our own code expired working connections after about an hour.** Intervals access tokens don't expire, and the response has no expiry time or refresh token. The server filled in a default of one hour anyway, then marked the connection \"expired, reconnect\" and deleted the token. Intervals never revoked anything. Now a connection with no expiry stays connected, and it's only marked expired if Intervals actually rejects the token (`server.py`).\n\nIn the local run, the request without a token still gets a 401 and the button's request returns the link with the new permission list. The callback saves the connection with no expiry, and it was still connected after several passes of the background token-refresh job.\n\nRivera's saved token was already deleted, so Rivera will need to click Connect once more after this ships. Nothing needs redoing on the Intervals side.\n\nSome other process was already using port 8766, so I ran my test server on 8788 and left that process alone.\n\nThe causes and fixes are written up in `ANSWER.md`, which also notes that the secrets in `config.py` are dev placeholders that prod must override. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1555,"cache_read_input_tokens":35995,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1555},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d834dbd0-921c-4e4a-824e-1bb7dc043d17","uuid":"92ced917-767d-4a35-996e-c2ba93a6b1d9","timestamp":"2026-10-27T05:27:43.893Z","request_id":"req_011CgSBtnzzf3tf2vbWANJrU"}
